From 7b62e17797fbcc0ba90af8bce39d1f36fc532b0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <services.claude@accounts.ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 21:38:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: patch 0134 -- one-time VBE 4F06 Get Scan Line Length
 probe

Piggybacked on SDL_HINT_DOS_BANK_CHUNK_PROBE activation, same as
SDL/0132 and SDL/0133. Calls INT 10h AX=4F06h BL=01h (VBE Get Logical
Scan Line Length) once and logs BytesPerScanLine/PixelsPerScanLine/
MaxScanLines from the BIOS directly beside fb_state.vram_pitch.

Why not just trust SDL/0133's CRTC[0x13] dump: that register's
documented convention (actual_stride = CRTC13 * 2) is a generic VGA
byte-mode default, not a guarantee -- some chips extend or reinterpret
it, so a mismatch there is ambiguous without knowing this chip's exact
multiplier. VBE function 4F06 is a standardized BIOS abstraction every
VBE-conformant card must implement; its Get subfunction reports the
BIOS's own understanding of BytesPerScanLine with no interpretation
required on our end. This backend's mode-set path (SDL_dosmodes.c)
currently takes BytesPerScanLine from the 4F01 GetModeInfo call at
mode-set time and never calls 4F06 (Set or Get) to reconfirm it -- if
the BIOS's own Get answer disagrees with that cached value, that is a
BIOS-internal inconsistency independent of both the CPU-bus readback
question (SDL/0128-0131) and the raw-register ambiguity (SDL/0133).

Read-only: BL=01h is the Get subfunction, no hardware state changes.

Verified via isolated standalone compile with stub dpmi/log functions
(gcc -Wall -Wextra -Werror, zero warnings) before generating this
patch, per this repo's patch-authoring convention.
---
 src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c b/src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c
index 05ef458af..6460a2602 100644
--- a/src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c
+++ b/src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c
@@ -1305,6 +1305,39 @@ static void dos_bank_chunk_probe_resolve(void)
             "backend's mode-set path)",
             crtc13, (unsigned)crtc13 * 2, fb_state.vram_pitch);
     }
+
+    // SDL/0134: one-time VBE function 4F06h Get Scan Line Length call
+    // (BL=01h), piggybacked on the same probe activation as SDL/0132-0133.
+    // Unlike SDL/0133's raw CRTC[0x13] read, this is a standardized VESA
+    // BIOS service every card's VBE implementation must answer -- no
+    // chip-specific multiplier guessing required, since the BIOS itself
+    // reports BytesPerScanLine in BX directly. This backend's mode-set
+    // path (SDL_dosmodes.c) calls neither the Set nor the Get subfunction
+    // of 4F06 -- it takes BytesPerScanLine from the earlier 4F01
+    // GetModeInfo call at mode-set time and never asks the BIOS to
+    // reconfirm it. If the BIOS's *own* Get answer here disagrees with
+    // that GetModeInfo-derived fb_state.vram_pitch, that is a
+    // BIOS-internal inconsistency independent of both the CPU-bus
+    // readback question (SDL/0128-0131) and the raw-CRTC-register
+    // multiplier ambiguity (SDL/0133) -- a definitive answer rather than
+    // another data point requiring interpretation. Read-only: BL=01h is
+    // the Get subfunction, no hardware state is modified by this call.
+    {
+        __dpmi_regs regs;
+        SDL_zero(regs);
+        regs.x.ax = 0x4F06;
+        regs.x.bx = 0x0001;
+        __dpmi_int(0x10, &regs);
+        dos_port_dbg_log(
+            "sdl: SDL/0134 VBE-4F06-GET-SCANLINE (read-only, INT 10h "
+            "AX=4F06h BL=01h) status=0x%04x BytesPerScanLine=%u "
+            "PixelsPerScanLine=%u MaxScanLines=%u vs vram_pitch=%d "
+            "(BIOS-declared BytesPerScanLine from the earlier 4F01 "
+            "GetModeInfo call at mode-set time, never reconfirmed "
+            "against this Get call by this backend's mode-set path)",
+            regs.x.ax, regs.x.bx, regs.x.cx, regs.x.dx,
+            fb_state.vram_pitch);
+    }
 }
 
 static void dos_bank_chunk_probe_maybe_log(const Uint8 *src, Uint32 src_off,
-- 
